      "geometry": "rect",
      "text": "\nKasemrat, R., & Kraiwanit, T. (2025) . [3]  With the growing complexity of consumer data, Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks have emerged as robust solutions for capturing temporal dependencies. and developed LSTM specifically to manage the long-term dependencies inherent in sequential data. This makes LSTM suitable for e-commerce, where customer behaviour evolves over time and requires nuanced understand. However, despite LSTM's predictive capabilities, its “black box” nature has driven researchers to enhance it with mechanisms that improve interpretability, such as Explainable AI (XAI) techniques.\n\nSharma, A., Patel, N., & Gupta, R. (2022). [7] provide an extensive evaluation of LSTM structures, underscoring their robust-ness in handling vanishing gradient problems common in traditional RNNs. Several studies have demonstrated LSTM’s capability in financial time series forecasting, showcasing improved performance over conventional methods. Tan, Y. (2025). [6] These studies indicate that the integration of visualization improves prediction accuracy while notably enhancing user trust and interpretability. This ﬁnding aligns with the approach in this study that combines multi-layer LSTM with dynamic heatmaps and feature weight visualization, conﬁrming the visualization tools’ keyrole in improving interpretability.\n\n",
